Title: Even Better
Credit: example micro-story for ai-movie
Draft: 4 (Dad's car planted: parking bay, engine sound, one light)

[[VOICE: a dry, deadpan Australian man, 40s to 50s, general Australian accent, low to mid pitch, close mic, unhurried. The humour is in restraint. Never a rising inflection on a statement; no smile until the last line. Marks: (breath) = breath point, | = pause without a breath.]]

EXT. APARTMENT BALCONY AND STREET - BLUE HOUR

[[beat b1 (ki): a quiet evening; this small person is waiting]]

From the street: a lit third-floor window. In front of it, a small figure on tiptoe at the balcony rail, chin just over the top. Below her, an empty parking bay at the kerb. The streetlight on the corner flickers on.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
(matter-of-fact, quietly fond; falling)
[[0.8 s in; breath; 0.5 s pause after o'clock; breath after watch]]
(breath) Six o'clock. | Mia keeps watch. (breath)

[[beat b2 (sho): we know what she wants]]

Close, through the balusters. MIA (6) leans to one side, peering down the street towards the corner, both hands on the rail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
(confiding; small lift on "knows", land "car" cleanly)
[[0.6 s in; no pause inside the line; long silent breath after]]
(breath) She knows the sound of Dad's car.

[[beat b3 (ten): hope, held in silence]]

An engine, out of sight, growing closer. Looking down from the balcony: a single bright light swings round the corner under the streetlight. Mia rises higher on her toes.

[[beat b4 (ten): not Dad]]

On the cut the engine turns into a scooter's whine. The light belongs to a delivery scooter, a pizza box strapped on the back, pulling up to her building.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
(deadpan, almost a shrug; flat, falling)
[[just after the brake squeak]]
Not Dad.

[[beat b5 (ketsu): delight]]

Mia throws both arms high above the rail, up on her toes, the warm window glowing behind her.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
(the smile finally audible; lift on "Even", settle on "better")
[[0.9 s in, after her arms go up; small audible breath first]]
(breath, small) Even better.

FADE OUT.
